Unless the exact number varies by at least an order of magnitude,
meatballs are not sustainable as a food source.  In a typical game
you will not see anywhere near 120 rocks until you get a pick-axe
and start breaking all the statues at Delphi, and at that point you
have almost made it to Sokoban, where food is going to become a much
smaller problem, typically.

IMO, the number of rocks in the dungeon should be increased, simply
because rocks are such a mundane, limited-value object.  I imagine
they are as scarce as they are primarily to prevent "large" save
game files (back in the era where a few kilobytes per file actually
mattered because 20 megabytes was a typical hard drive size, and
keychain fobs good for hundreds of megabytes of storage did not
exist much less come free in specially marked boxes of breakfast
cereal).  This is no longer a significant concern.  Even the most
resource-scarce handheld devices these days could yawn at a mere
hundred-megabyte save-game file, especially for a game where you
typically only have one save file stored at a time.  At this point
I can't see any reason for there not to be a couple hundred rocks
on the ground *per level* (average), if only for atmosphere.
Also, cavemen could really stand to have some sling ammo available
from time to time, and it's not like rocks are overpowered.

Eating meatballs for nutrition would still be tedious, though.


